winebus: ask the motors the way Sony's library asks them, on request.

A DualSense knows two ways to be told to vibrate. LEGACY is flag0 bit 0x01,
which SDL calls "Enable rumble emulation": the pad drives its voice coils in
imitation of a pair of rotating-mass motors. HAPTIC is flag2 bit 0x04, SDL's
"Enable improved rumble emulation", the branch its PS5 driver takes for
firmware 2.24 and above. Both also set flag0 bit 0x02, which is why that bit is
named for what it does -- disable audio haptics -- and not for a path.

mgvf-0010 has always sent LEGACY. The reason is in mgvf-0009's header: on a
six-pulse ladder by hand, legacy at 255 felt clearly stronger than haptic at
255. That is one person's hand and it is the whole of the evidence, and it was
gathered when this driver could deliver about sixteen updates a second, because
anything faster queued behind the game's own writes and cost it frames.

mgvf-0020 removed that limit. The motors now ride the game's own packet at its
own rate -- sixty a second on the title measured -- so the comparison is worth
making again under conditions the ladder never had. XInputRumbleHapticPath, off
by default, is that comparison: one registry value, both writers, no rebuild
between the two runs.

WHAT SONY'S LIBRARY DOES, since it is the reference. In the Bluetooth session
captured here it sets flag0 0x02 and flag2 0x04 on all 8,608 of its packets --
including the 96.5% that carry no motor -- and never once sets bit 0x01. Every
native title measured here takes the haptic path.

TWO OPTIONS THAT CONTRADICT EACH OTHER, said plainly because the driver cannot
resolve it. mgvf-0009's VibrationMode 1 exists to rewrite a client's haptic
choice into the legacy motors, and it runs on the packet AFTER the stamp. So
asking for both is asking for opposite things and the rewrite wins; a WARN at
device arrival says so. To compare the paths, leave the vibration at the game's
own choice.

WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED: which path feels better at sixty updates a second.
That is what the option is for, and no claim is made here about the outcome.
The thread's own packet takes the same path as the ride, so the two writers
cannot disagree -- except on a report too short to carry flag2 at byte 39,
where it keeps the legacy path rather than selecting nothing at all.

Signed-off-by: MacGameVideoFix
---
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.h
@@ -132,6 +132,25 @@
 #define DUALSENSE_FLAG0_DISABLE_AUDIO_HAPTICS 0x02
 #define DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG1           1
 
+/* MGVF mgvf-0021: the two ways a packet can ask the motors to move, named
+ * after what the pad does rather than after which is "better".
+ *
+ * LEGACY is flag0 bit 0x01, SDL's "Enable rumble emulation": the pad puts its
+ * voice coils into a mode that imitates a pair of rotating-mass motors.
+ * HAPTIC is flag2 bit 0x04, SDL's "Enable improved rumble emulation", the
+ * branch its PS5 driver takes for firmware 2.24 and above. BOTH also set flag0
+ * bit 0x02 -- SDL sets it on either branch and so does Sony's own library on
+ * all 8,608 packets of the session captured here, which is why that bit is
+ * named for what it is and not for the path it belongs to.
+ *
+ * mgvf-0010 has always sent LEGACY, on the strength of a six-pulse ladder in
+ * which it felt stronger than HAPTIC at the same value. That ladder was run
+ * when this driver could only deliver about sixteen updates a second; at the
+ * sixty a second mgvf-0020 now delivers the comparison is worth making again,
+ * which is what XInputRumbleHapticPath is for. */
+#define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY 0
+#define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC 1
+
 /* MGVF mgvf-0016: the shortest output report that carries the whole of what
  * the rewrite reads. flag2 is the last of the four bytes and it is common byte
  * 38, so a USB report has to reach index 39 and therefore be 40 bytes long.
@@ -289,7 +308,7 @@
 int dualsense_bt_output_is_inert(const unsigned char *report, unsigned int len);
 unsigned int dualsense_bt_stamp_motors(unsigned char *report, unsigned int len,
                                        unsigned char right, unsigned char left,
-                                       dualsense_crc32_fn crc32);
+                                       unsigned int path, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32);
 
 /* In place. Each returns len when the report was changed and 0 when it was
  * left as it was: too short to carry a CRC tail at all, or, for the read, the
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_usb.c
@@ -570,7 +570,7 @@
 
 unsigned int dualsense_bt_stamp_motors(unsigned char *report, unsigned int len,
                                        unsigned char right, unsigned char left,
-                                       dualsense_crc32_fn crc32)
+                                       unsigned int path, dualsense_crc32_fn crc32)
 {
     static const unsigned char seed = DUALSENSE_CRC_SEED_OUTPUT;
     unsigned char *common = report + DUALSENSE_BT_COMMON;
@@ -578,7 +578,13 @@
 
     if (len != DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_LEN || report[0] != DUALSENSE_BT_REPORT_ID) return 0;
 
-    common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG0] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG0_LEGACY_VIBRATION | DUALSENSE_FLAG0_DISABLE_AUDIO_HAPTICS;
+    /* MGVF mgvf-0021: 0x02 on both paths, because SDL sets it on both and so
+     * does Sony's library; only the selecting bit differs. */
+    common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG0] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG0_DISABLE_AUDIO_HAPTICS;
+    if (path == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC)
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG2] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG2_HAPTIC_VIBRATION;
+    else
+        common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_FLAG0] |= DUALSENSE_FLAG0_LEGACY_VIBRATION;
     common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_RIGHT] = right;
     common[DUALSENSE_COMMON_MOTOR_LEFT] = left;
 
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_haptics.h
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_haptics.h
@@ -109,6 +109,15 @@
  * is 1.6% of a motor's range. Measured on Beast of Reincarnation: 3-5 fps and
  * visible stutter became 2 fps and none, with the rumble unchanged to the hand
  * that had been complaining about it. */
+/* MGVF mgvf-0021: the motor path, mirrored from dualsense_usb.h so this header
+ * stands on its own whichever order a translation unit includes them in. The
+ * two files are compiled together and a mismatch would be caught at once, but
+ * the definition belongs beside the flag bits it selects, which are there. */
+#ifndef DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY
+#define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY 0
+#define DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC 1
+#endif
+
 #define DUALSENSE_HAPTICS_BT_DEADBAND      4
 
 /* MGVF mgvf-0020: how long the client has to be silent before the haptics
@@ -202,7 +211,7 @@
 unsigned int dualsense_haptics_motor_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
                                             unsigned int length,
                                             unsigned int rumble, unsigned int buzz,
-                                            unsigned int gain);
+                                            unsigned int gain, unsigned int path);
 
 /* MGVF mgvf-0010, second half: describe the fields of the pad's Bluetooth input report.
  *
--- a/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_haptics.c
+++ b/dlls/winebus.sys/dualsense_haptics.c
@@ -455,6 +455,12 @@
  * without it, the request is accepted and nothing is felt: the pad has the
  * effect but not through the motors. */
 #define HAPTICS_OUT_LEGACY_BIT   (0x01 | 0x02)
+/* MGVF mgvf-0021: the other path. 0x02 stays on either way -- see
+ * dualsense_usb.h -- and the selecting bit moves to flag2, which lives at
+ * common byte 38 and so needs a report long enough to hold it. */
+#define HAPTICS_OUT_AUDIO_BIT    0x02
+#define HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG2        39
+#define HAPTICS_OUT_HAPTIC_BIT   0x04
 
 /* A percentage over one motor byte, saturating rather than wrapping: asking for
  * twice full power is asking for full power, not for none. */
@@ -470,14 +476,24 @@
 unsigned int dualsense_haptics_motor_report(unsigned char *out, unsigned int out_size,
                                             unsigned int length,
                                             unsigned int rumble, unsigned int buzz,
-                                            unsigned int gain)
+                                            unsigned int gain, unsigned int path)
 {
     if (length <= HAPTICS_OUT_MOTOR_LEFT) return 0;
     if (out_size < length) return 0;
+    /* The haptic path's bit is at byte 39; a report too short to carry it
+     * cannot select that path, so it keeps the legacy one rather than sending
+     * a packet that selects nothing and buzzes not at all. */
+    if (path == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC && length <= HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG2)
+        path = DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_LEGACY;
 
     memset(out, 0, length);
     out[0] = DUALSENSE_HAPTICS_MOTOR_REPORT_ID;
-    out[HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG0] = HAPTICS_OUT_LEGACY_BIT;
+    if (path == DUALSENSE_VIBRATION_PATH_HAPTIC)
+    {
+        out[HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG0] = HAPTICS_OUT_AUDIO_BIT;
+        out[HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG2] = HAPTICS_OUT_HAPTIC_BIT;
+    }
+    else out[HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG0] = HAPTICS_OUT_LEGACY_BIT;
     out[HAPTICS_OUT_FLAG1] = 0;
     out[HAPTICS_OUT_MOTOR_RIGHT] = haptics_scale(buzz >> 8, gain);
     out[HAPTICS_OUT_MOTOR_LEFT] = haptics_scale(rumble >> 8, gain);
